// already emitted "<<". Inside the span, the parser is the hard authority: we
// use adaptive/group-aware constrained steps while there is budget, but if EOS
// is produced inside the span or remaining budget becomes too small before a
// complete expression is reached, we salvage validity by rolling back to a
// valid prefix and then appending a minimal grammar-valid fallback token "0"
// when allowed, closing the span as soon as the parser reports completeness.
// This supports the task because natural-language reasoning stays outside the
// constrained region, while the final visible <<...>> span is kept valid and
// closed even under uncertainty.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity: In unconstrained generation, the implication is vacuous
//   unless we enter a span. The explicit open branch uses OpenConstrainedSpan,
//   and the observed-open branch uses EnterObservedConstrainedSpan; both set
//   currentConstrainedOut := [], which is valid by parser.IsValidPrefix([]).
//   In the close branch, CloseConstrainedSpan sets insideConstrainedOut to
//   false, so the implication becomes vacuous. In the ordinary constrained-step
//   branch, AdaptiveConstrainedStep returns either EOS or a parser-valid next
//   token, and AppendConstrainedToken preserves parser validity. In the salvage
//   branch, RollbackConstrainedSuffix returns a valid constrained prefix; if
//   token "0" is valid next, AppendConstrainedToken preserves validity, and if
//   not, the rolled-back valid prefix is retained. Thus every branch preserves
//   insideConstrainedOut ==> parser.IsValidPrefix(currentConstrainedOut).
// progress: The free-token branch consumes one step via UnconstrainedStep and
//   appends at most one visible token. OpenConstrainedSpan and
//   CloseConstrainedSpan each consume one step and append at most one visible
//   delimiter. EnterObservedConstrainedSpan consumes zero steps and appends no
//   token, so the bound is unchanged. The constrained-step branch consumes one
//   step and appends at most one visible token. The salvage branch may perform
//   rollback at zero cost, then optionally append one fallback token using
//   AppendConstrainedToken after a single AdaptiveConstrainedStep-equivalent
//   budget increment we account for directly, and may then close with one more
